      "fps": {
        "value": 1,
        "unit": "frames per second",
        "origin": "SET",
        "note": "ONE FRAME PER SECOND, AND THE RATE IS AN INTEGER BECAUSE THE HARNESS REQUIRES ONE: a frame is `index / fps` seconds and Blender's own rate is whole. A rate below one -- which is what sampling a 60 s span in five steps would need -- has no spelling, so the grid is a second apart and the note below says what it therefore covers."
      },
      "frames": {
        "value": 5,
        "unit": "frames",
        "origin": "derived",
        "derivation": "min(5, ceil(34.8800011 s) + 1) = 5, so the grid runs 0..4 s.",
        "note": "WHAT THIS GRID COVERS, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED. The file's declared span is 34.8800011 s and this case samples its first 4 s. Where the span is shorter than that, glTF clamps past the last keyframe and the tail frames state the end pose rather than an extrapolation. Where it is LONGER -- and it is for the long ones -- this case exercises the OPENING of the motion and says so: it decides that the animation is read, applied and posed, not that the whole of it is right. A case that needed the whole span would declare a grid for it and pay for the frames."
      }
